## 1. Attack on the certificate — item by item (FOR_CLAUDE_NEXT 1–3)

**1. Taylor expansion of both substeps and the gauge; cubic projection = normal-form coefficient.** *Accepted.* I re-derived symbolically (sympy, exact) that the on-site substep expands as `Re = u + εu(k−u²) + (1+ε(k−3u²))ξ − ε(3uξ² + uη² + ξ³ + ξη²)`, `Im = (1+ε(k−u²))η − ε(2uξη + ξ²η + η³)`; adding the linear coupling gives exactly GPT's `X = u + J_Rξ − ε(…)`, `Y = Mη − ε(…)` (the constant collapses to `u` only at the host, and the certificate evaluates over the certified host box, so the enclosure is of the exact host's map). The sync substep on the positive chart (`atan(Y/X)`, `R cos h`, `R sin h`) and the gauge rotation by `−ϑ` are the same maps I used. Because every angle series has exactly zero constant term (I checked that the zero is an exact `IV(0,0)`, not a rounded one), `atan z = z − z³/3`, `sin z = z − z³/6`, `cos z = 1 − z²/2` are exact modulo `t⁴`. Convention: with embedding `z(a) = aq + a²w/2 + a³h₃`, `Aq = −q`, `Aᵀp = −p`, `⟨p,q⟩ = 1`, the `a³` projection of `F̂(z(a))` on `p` is `⟨p,C(q,q,q)⟩/6 + ⟨p,B(q,w)⟩/2 + ⟨p,Ah₃⟩`, and `⟨p,Ah₃⟩ = −⟨p,h₃⟩ = 0`; the `a²` consistency gives `(I−A)w = B(q,q)`; `⟨p,w⟩ = 0` holds automatically because `B(q,q)` lies in the `H`-even (`ξ`) block while `p` is `H`-odd. So the cubic coefficient read from the second path **is** the Kuznetsov flip coefficient in my convention (same `|q| = 1`, `p = q` normalisation, hence directly comparable: GPT `0.0865248497217875384733…`, my finite-difference value `0.086524849721787538473`).

**2. Does the integer-interval code enclose everything?** *Accepted after inspection of each rounding operation.* `IV.make` floors/ceils the exact rational (decimal strings, `1/20`, `1/5`, `1e-30`, `0.7` are converted from `Fraction`, never from a rounded float); `__mul__` takes min/max of the four endpoint products with floor/ceil; `recip` uses `floor(S²/hi)`, `ceil(S²/lo)` and is correct for negative intervals (I tested `[−7/3, −1/5]`) and raises on zero-containing ones; `__pow__(2)` handles zero-straddling intervals (`lo = 0`), higher powers fall back to repeated multiplication (valid, looser); `sqrt` uses `isqrt` with a `+1` correction on the upper end (tested with `lo = 0` and non-square endpoints); addition is exact. Jet division and square root are the formal coefficient recurrences (I verified the Jet class against `mp.taylor` on generic rational data to `< 10⁻²⁵`). The residual is evaluated at an **exact dyadic centre** with rational-interval parameters (this answers GPT's valid qualification of my own earlier `mpi(Fu)` wrapping); the basis `V`, `K = VᵀMV`, `m₁, m₂`, `q`, `η`, `λ_c`, `σ`, `(I−J_R)⁻¹` (cofactor/determinant) and the coefficient are all evaluated over the host **box**, so the exact host's values are enclosed (dependency between repeated occurrences of the box only widens the result). The eigenvector `(B, m₁−A)` is nonzero (its norm interval would otherwise trip `recip`). Nothing in the chain uses a floating value as an authority.

**3. Contraction / Sylvester arguments.** *Accepted.* `K(X) ⊂ int X` with `‖I − Y·Df(X)‖∞ < 1` and `det Y ≠ 0` gives existence and uniqueness of the zero in `X` (Krawczyk–Moore); the contraction bound `1.8·10⁻³⁰` is enormous margin. `J_R` is symmetric (`M` symmetric), so positive leading principal minors of `J_R` and of `0.7I − J_R` give `spec(J_R) ⊂ (0, 0.7)`, hence `I − J_R` invertible and the real block stable; `0 < m₂ < m₁ < 1` enclosed gives the simple `−1` with the second multiplier `−m₂/m₁ ∈ (−1, 0)` and `σ = 9m₁ > 0`.

**Verdict on the candidate certificate:** `0.0865248497217 < c < 0.0865248497219` is **accepted** for the exact-decimal parameters `ε = 1/20`, `g = 1/5`, `k = (1, 1.2208964704604097, 6.35310346037241)` at `λ_c` of the certified host. Together with the enclosed simple crossing and `σ > 0` it closes my gap **G1**: the local flip theorem now holds with all hypotheses certified. Scope unchanged: no neighbourhood radius, no `μ`-range, no basin (G2), no continuation to `λ = 0.5`, no binary64 statement, no physical identification.

